  <h2>Hear the relationship in natural U.S. speech</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In a short condition-first sentence, speakers often make two thought groups: <span class="ex">If the CARD is expired, / the reader flashes RED.</span> The comma marks the clause boundary in writing; a small pause can mark it in speech. Important content words carry stress. Function words such as <span class="ex">if, the, a</span> are usually lighter.</p>
  <p>Do not reduce the third-person ending away. <span class="ex">Scans, unlocks, flashes, sends</span> still need their final agreement sound. In result-first order, the sentence may flow as one thought group: <span class="ex">The door stays LOCKED if the card is inACTIVE.</span></p>

