  <h2>Communicate: explain a process someone can use</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Choose one route:</b> explain a real process you know well, or invent a fictional process so you do not have to share private information. Possible topics include joining a video call, handling a customer request, opening or closing a workplace, using a device, preparing a familiar meal, or managing a personal routine.</div>
  <div class="speak-box">
    <h3>Process specialist and new user</h3>
    <p><b>Specialist:</b> Give a short overview, then explain at least four condition-result relationships. Include one expected stage with <span class="ex">when</span>, one possible case with <span class="ex">if</span>, one negative condition, and one result-first sentence. State the limits of your knowledge. If one result concerns only tomorrow or another single future case, use a First Conditional and explain why.</p>
    <p><b>New user:</b> Ask follow-up questions: <span class="ex">What happens if...? Does that happen every time? What do I do when...? Is that true for every version? What will happen if it changes tomorrow?</span></p>
    <p><b>Switch roles:</b> The second specialist explains a different process. The listener challenges one sentence that sounds too broad or has an unclear time relationship.</p>
    <p><b>Tutor monitors:</b> present-simple agreement in both clauses, clear repeated meaning, purposeful <span class="ex">if / when</span>, accurate Zero-versus-First contrast, and evidence limits.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="writing-card">
    <h3>Write the quick guide</h3>
    <p>Write 90–120 words for a new user. Include four Zero Conditionals, one condition-first sentence, one result-first sentence, one negative condition, and one limited claim such as <span class="ex">In this version...</span> or <span class="ex">For my normal routine...</span>. Add one First Conditional only if you describe a genuinely open future case.</p>
  </div>

